  * `selector_or_locator` - A selector or locator to use when resolving DOM element.

  ## Options

  * `:has` (`t:PlaywrightEx.Locator.t/0`) - Narrows down the results of the method to those which contain elements matching this relative locator.
    For example, `article` that has `text=Playwright` matches `<article><div>Playwright</div></article>`.

    Inner locator **must be relative** to the outer locator and is queried starting with the outer locator match, not the document root. For example, you can find `content` that has `div` in `<article><content><div>Playwright</div></content></article>`. However, looking for `content` that has `article div` will fail, because the inner locator must be relative and should not use any elements outside the `content`.

    Note that outer and inner locators must belong to the same frame. Inner locator must not contain [`FrameLocator`](`PlaywrightEx.FrameLocator`)s.
  * `:has_not` (`t:PlaywrightEx.Locator.t/0`) - *Since Playwright 1.33.* Matches elements that do not contain an element that matches an inner locator. Inner locator is queried against the outer one.
    For example, `article` that does not have `div` matches `<article><span>Playwright</span></article>`.

    Note that outer and inner locators must belong to the same frame. Inner locator must not contain [`FrameLocator`](`PlaywrightEx.FrameLocator`)s.
  * `:has_not_text` (`t:String.t/0` | `t:Regex.t/0` | `t:PlaywrightEx.RegExp.t/0`) - *Since Playwright 1.33.* Matches elements that do not contain specified text somewhere inside, possibly in a child or a descendant element. When passed a `string`, matching is case-insensitive and searches for a substring.
  * `:has_text` (`t:String.t/0` | `t:Regex.t/0` | `t:PlaywrightEx.RegExp.t/0`) - Matches elements containing specified text somewhere inside, possibly in a child or a descendant element. When passed a `string`, matching is case-insensitive and searches for a substring.
    For example, `"Playwright"` matches `<article><div>Playwright</div></article>`.
  """
  @spec locator(t(), String.t() | PlaywrightEx.Locator.t(), locator_options()) :: PlaywrightEx.Locator.t()
  def locator(frame_locator, selector_or_locator, options \\ []) do
    options = Facade.options!(options, @locator_options, "frameLocator.locator")
    PlaywrightEx.Client.FrameLocator.locator(frame_locator, selector_or_locator, options)
  end

  @doc deprecated: "Use PlaywrightEx.Locator.nth/2 followed by PlaywrightEx.Locator.content_frame/1 instead"
  @doc ~S"""
  Returns locator to the n-th matching frame. It's zero based, `nth(0)` selects the first frame.

  *Since Playwright 1.17.*
  """
  @spec nth(t(), integer()) :: PlaywrightEx.FrameLocator.t()
  def nth(frame_locator, index) do
    PlaywrightEx.Client.FrameLocator.nth(frame_locator, index)
  end

  @doc ~S"""
  Returns a [`Locator`](`PlaywrightEx.Locator`) object pointing to the same `iframe` as this frame locator.

  Useful when you have a [`FrameLocator`](`PlaywrightEx.FrameLocator`) object obtained somewhere, and later on would like to interact with the `iframe` element.

  For a reverse operation, use `PlaywrightEx.Locator.content_frame/1`.

  **Usage**

  ```elixir
  frame_locator = Page.locator(page, ~s(iframe[name="embedded"])) |> Locator.content_frame()
  # ...
  locator = FrameLocator.owner(frame_locator)
  Expect.to_be_visible!(locator)
  ```

  *Since Playwright 1.43.*
  """
  @spec owner(t()) :: PlaywrightEx.Locator.t()
  def owner(frame_locator) do
    PlaywrightEx.Client.FrameLocator.owner(frame_locator)
  end

